//
// FPM BENCH — a REPRODUCIBLE frame-time measurement. Same place, same hour, every run.
//
// ★ WHY, and it is a measured failure rather than a feature idea.
//
// On 2026-08-09 an entire afternoon of manual A/B produced six wrong conclusions before naming a
// 20 ms cvar. The measurements were not wrong because the instrument was bad — the hitch meter was
// fine. They were wrong because FOUR VARIABLES MOVED between samples and nobody held them:
//
//   * CAMERA POSITION. "Face the platform" is not a position. Two readings framed differently are
//     two different scenes, and I compared them as if they were one.
//   * TIME OF DAY. A live day/night cycle changed the directional light's contribution mid-session.
//     The plan did not list it as a variable at all; it invalidated an hour.
//   * DURATION. Instantaneous FPS off a screenshot, n=1, read by eye.
//   * ORDER. Console writes stick and outrank scalability, so tests contaminated their successors.
//
// Ant, on being handed yet another list of steps: *"automate as much as possible by default. i dont
// like running around throwing commands around."* She is right, and the deeper reason is that a
// human cannot hold those four still. Code can.
//
// YOU MARK THE PLACES THAT HURT ONCE. After that the bench flies the route itself.
//
// ⚠⚠ THIS ONE CAN WRITE TO HER SAVE, WHICH NOTHING ELSE IN FPM DOES.
// `AFGTimeOfDaySubsystem` implements `IFGSaveInterface` (FGTimeSubsystem.h:19) — the time of day is
// SAVED WITH THE WORLD. So is the player's position. Every other FPM surface touches cvars, which
// are session state and evaporate; this touches persistent state. The whole design below is shaped
// by that: original position, rotation and clock are captured BEFORE anything moves, and restored on
// EVERY exit path — completion, abort, world teardown, module shutdown. If it cannot guarantee
// restoration it does not start.

